what are the best ways for directors and  actors to unpack a screenplay  you know the first thing is that  you want to meet and talk with the actor  as a director  and before you cast them you hopefully  and um  and I'm talking about in terms of like  stars who are going to be made an offer  you're not going to chance to  necessarily audition them or but if  you're if you're dealing with a name  sort of actor you want to make sure you  can sit and talk with them and just know  you you're going to make the same movie  uh because it's really a bummer when you  get on the set and you realize this  actor sees it a whole other movie and  you may not win that battle because they  are oftentimes more important to the  studio than the directors are so  um so it's important to have the  dialogue going and just be sure like we  see this movie the same way and we feel  his character the same way  um so that that's an important thing and  most good actors will do their own  breakdowns used to be like well years  ago when I was getting started I used to  write biographies for every character  I'd written you know way into their  childhoods where I formed them I've  always been weeks and weeks and weeks  doing this they were given to the actor  and I would say look this is not a Bible  um this is just something for you to  reference and if there's something you  want to use great if not throw it away  and usually they were grateful sometimes  they were like yeah okay  but I've learned now that good actors  will do that already and they want to  bring their own thing to this  character's backstory they don't  necessarily want my take the writer's  take or the director's take they want to  find their own way into this character  the same way I have to find my own way  into a story  so unless it's something really critical  about that backstory that that informs  you know an important behavioral point  in the in the movie  um I don't do that anymore because I  know they're gonna They're Gonna Fill In  Those blanks for themselves  and uh uh you know I think really it's  just about discussion most of all you  know I do rehearse but for me a valid  rehearsal is just as much just sitting  over dinner and just talking about where  we think this character has been and  what we think they want all those things  as a writer you do  um where the characters going and  um we just sort of discuss different  approaches and uh so you know hopefully  by the time you start shooting you have  answered each other's questions about  the direction of this character how this  character is going to become you know  come to life on screen and so you know  from there I don't tend to do  heavy blocking rehearsals like she knew  met famously rehearses for two weeks  like a play you know marks out the  locations the props you know furniture  and he has the actors go through every  scene start to finish like a play and  that works great for him and obviously  you know soon the movies are fantastic  um I don't want to  pin it down that much and also I've  rarely had the luxury of time I mean you  know I'm trying to make a sound like  I've made a choice about the rehearsal  but really it's mostly because I only  get a couple of days tops and I want to  rehearse with the yeah I don't really do  we do like a table read with everybody  but then I want to break them up into  groups  um and and just kind of work more  intimately with them uh and so you know  a lot of this discussion and how to  um what what the big moments are in the  script uh we'd like to talk about a  little bit about the scenes with the  actors and I like to ask them questions  um I like to instead of coming in and  saying well here's what I think about  this character I think this guy I like  to say well what do you think you're  after in the scene  you know and I like to say what do you  think is the most important thing you  say in the scene  and I learn a lot from that because  oftentimes it's not what I think at all  but it's better than what I thought or  just different in a way that I can say  this is where he's going to go with that  that's great  um so I I tend to now just be much more  interested in what the actor feels about  it uh and and what their take is on  where a scene is going to go or uh and  you know just getting off the your the  topic of your question for a bit but  just another casting you know it's so  important because of this process to  cast that right actor you know and and  you're the old joke it's 50 direct job  whatever it is but it's it's like 90 of  the director's job you get that right  actor in that role  um because it's not my job as a director  to teach someone how to act or to teach  them how to be in this role  um they got to come with their game and  they're gonna they're gonna play to win  and so you've got to be sure that that  actor is is sees it the way you do and  is going to give you what you what the  what the role needs because if not you  know you really can't reasonably expect  the actor to completely okay no I need  you this is all wrong it'll come come at  this from a completely different  direction  that's rarely going to work you know  most actors are just not going to  respond to that and so you're sort of  stuck with what what their  interpretation so casting most important  thing in the world to do  and with this back story that you would  prepare it sounds like a lot of it maybe  the audience wasn't even privy to some  of what it was the most none of it you  know it's really just about even the  actor a sense of uh their own context  but I don't do it anymore like I said  because it's just the actors kind of do  for themselves and I don't want them to  feel like I'm imposing this idea on them  you know if they need something for me  they know they can come to me and say  what do you think you know then that's  that's great but for the most part uh I  I trust them to you know feed them their  characters out  what's your process for reading a  screenplay that someone else wrote  um you know I try to imagine myself as  the viewer  um and uh yeah I try just to clear my  mind of all the things that I know about  how movies are made and just try to  concentrate on the story and the  characters I usually have to read a  script twice to really get it  um uh and if I if I choose to do it then  you know many more times  but sometimes I'm more able to  find flaws in other people's scripts  than in my own  so I can so a friend of mine gives me a  script to read I can give him fantastic  notes and feedback but I can't do it on  my own script I have to have somebody  else okay and tell me where I've gone  wrong there's a strange blindness that  you get  what's the wrong way for a director to  read a screenplay  I guess you know I mean there's probably  a couple of wrong ways but uh  the the first one way is to read it like  a person in the film business  you know you want to try to read it like  an audience which is difficult because  it's got nomenclature and technology and  then you know words of technical stuff  in it that doesn't make it feel like  it's a reader-friendly experience  but um The Challenge I think is to try  to just forget everything you know and  read this thing and just have a visceral  emotional reaction to it do I feel for  these characters do I want to tell the  story do I want to find out what happens  after this is over  those are the kinds of correct things uh  you know to bring to it so I guess uh  the incorrect things are you know just  if you're too conscious of the process  that's a big scene man how am I going to  do that that's like a thousand extras  you know they're gonna give you the  money to do that you know I'm gonna need  a crane here you know that's for later  um you just want to try to you can do I  connect with this on any kind of human  level you know do I care about these  people  so then the latter is how someone in the  industry would an industry read would go  instead of yeah for lack of a better  term I realized this sounds very woo-woo  but a heart read I guess so yeah yeah  that's a good way to put it that's you  know it really is you you hope when I  write a script I I use very few  technical terms uh I make it very spare  uh my paragraphs no more than four lines  each and I never I open up a script and  I see a paragraph this long oh God it's  a dense it's hard to read so I try to  make everything spare reader friendly  and I try to be careful not to ever  describe something that can't be  photographed  you know I don't like to say you know  this character he walks in the room and  uh she feels totally out of place  what does that mean you know what's the  behavior that tells us she feels solely  out of place is she checking yourself in  the mirror a little bit dude that's the  kind of thing I want to put in my script  and not just leave or directly to figure  out like how direct is going to be me so  I especially don't want to have to you  know figure that out so that's the way I  always tell writers you know just don't  write something you can't photograph  and um so hopefully if someone reads my  script they're not going to be too  bogged down in shots and all the  technical stuff that gets in the way of  the storytelling in that format
 

